    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d21">

                <pb facs="0098" n="22" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg22"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4231/1236: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Canada</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-09-09T15:00:00-04:00">September 9, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>94. Dr. Skelton<anchor n="13" xml:id="d21-f1"/> informs me that the Canadian
                    Government’s official list of articles on which the same [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] reductions under trade agreement will be
                    presented by Canadian Legation Tuesday September 10th. He informs me that list
                    differs somewhat from that already informally discussed, consideration having
                    been given to arguments presented by our representatives. Also certain articles
                    which had not occurred to our Government have been added.</p>
                <p>The concessions which the Canadians are prepared to make on American products are
                    probably being discussed here but they hope to have this list ready by the end
                    of the week before the Prime Minister leaves on his election speaking tour. In
                    studying our list he states that due consideration here also has been given to
                    the arguments ably advanced by our representatives.</p>
                <p>Three notes exchanged between Governments were published by the press this
                    morning. While local press reaction appears fair, Montreal <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gazette</hi> considers the action of Congress in
                    reimposing excise tax on Canadian lumber was a death blow to the negotiations.
                    Skelton assures me attitude of <hi rendition="#italic">Gazette</hi> should not
                    be taken seriously. Although the leading conservative organ it has consistently
                    opposed the Prime Minister’s reform program and is of course well known as
                    favoring a strong protective policy.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
